# 2026-05-19 — Today I Learned: structuredClone and Transferables

- structuredClone(value[, options]) performs a deep clone (including circular refs) of many built-ins (Date, Map, Set, RegExp, BigInt, Error, etc.).
- It does NOT clone functions or DOM nodes (those will throw).
- You can transfer ownership of Transferable objects (like ArrayBuffer, MessagePort) to avoid copying.

```js
// Deep clone example:
const original = { d: new Date(), s: new Set([1,2]), self: null };
original.self = original;
const copy = structuredClone(original);
console.log(copy.d instanceof Date, copy.self === copy); // true, true

// Transfer ArrayBuffer (neuters source)
const buf = new ArrayBuffer(8);
const view = new Uint8Array(buf);
view[0] = 42;
const cloned = structuredClone(buf, { transfer: [buf] });
console.log(cloned.byteLength, buf.byteLength); // 8, 0 (source detached)

// Caveats:
// - Functions and many host/DOM objects will throw when cloning.
// - Use transfer for zero-copy when passing large buffers between contexts.
```